{"name":"Wild Clary","hex":"#93a3c1","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wild","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Untamed; not domesticated.","Especially, being of the wild type: being of an unbroken ancestral line of undomesticated animals, as opposed to being feral, being an undomesticated animal whose ancestors were domesticated."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Inaccurately; not on target.","Intended to be synchronized with film or video but recorded separately."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wild"},{"term":"clary","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["clary sage (Salvia sclarea)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/clary"}],"entries":[{"title":"Salvia verbenaca","blocks":[{"text":"Salvia verbenaca, also known as wild clary or wild sage, is native to the British Isles, the Mediterranean region in Southern Europe, North Africa, and Near East, and in the Caucasus. It can be found as an introduced species that has naturalized in many parts of the world, including the eastern United States, California, Mexico, Argentina, Uruguay, southern Africa, Australia, New Zealand and China."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Salvia_verbenaca_b.JPG/330px-Salvia_verbenaca_b.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salvia_verbenaca","wikidata":"Q1313052"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Salvia_verbenaca_MHNT.BOT.2010.12.5.jpg/500px-Salvia_verbenaca_MHNT.BOT.2010.12.5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Salvia_verbenaca_MHNT.BOT.2010.12.5.jpg/1280px-Salvia_verbenaca_MHNT.BOT.2010.12.5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Salvia_verbenaca_MHNT.BOT.2010.1